%[text] %[text:anchor:H_6C7C5301] ## Transverse-shear locking due incompatible discretization spaces
%[text] The transverse-shear locking can be attributed to the incompatibility of the discretization space for the vertical displacement and rotation fields. This manifests as residual transverse-shear forces for thin plates which trigger this numerical artifact. More information on this topic you can find [here](file:..\4_TransverseShearLocking_TimoshenkoBeam\main_Chapter4_TransverseShearLocking_TimoshenkoBeam.m) based on the Timoshenko beam problem.
%[text] In this section the shear forces $q\_x = \\alpha G t \\gamma\_x = \\alpha G t \\overbrace{\\left( \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial X} + \\beta\_y \\right)}^{\\gamma\_x}$are computed using both the bilinear and the biquadratic elements along the line $Y = 2$ m.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_058221EE] ### Number of evaluation points
numPts = 100; %[control:slider:32fa]{"position":[10,13]}
%[text] ![](text:image:3b84) **Try**
%[text] Modify the number of evaluation points by means of variable `numPts`
%[text] ![](text:image:0ad2) **Reflect**
%[text] - How do the different distributions of the transverse-shear force change with respect to the number of evaluation points and why?
%[text] - How many evaluation points do you need to appropriately describe the different transverse-shear force distributions? Is this value constant or does it depend on the chosen value for the beam's edge size and thickness, $L$ and $t$, respectively? \
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8421377A] ### Transverse-shear force distribution along the the plate's section
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_5A961381] Navigate to [THIS](file:.\main_Chapter41_ReissnerMindlinSquarePlate_ShearForces.m) Live Script for the detailed analysis.
main_Chapter41_ReissnerMindlinSquarePlate_ShearForces %[output:49a4170a]
